 1 big thing: Corruption consensus 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : Gallup. Chart: Avery Lotz/Axios Americans' perceptions of government corruption have hit a two-decade high during President Trump's second term, Axios' Josephine Walker reports from a new Gallup survey. 

 🌀 Why it matters: The data underscores how deeply Americans' confidence in the federal government has eroded. By the numbers: 89% of U.S. adults say government corruption is widespread, Gallup found . 

 That's up 10 points from last year and a jump from the 72%–79% range between 2010 and 2025. Democrats are driving most of the rise, jumping to 91% this year after sitting at 76% during the first year of Trump's second term — when he reported some $2.2 billion in gains. 🌎 Americans are outliers among citizens in other developed nations: Among OECD member countries, the median level of perceived government corruption has fallen from 69% in 2009 to below 60% since 2022. 

 For the first time, the U.S. tops perceived corruption ratings among OECD countries. Since 2023, just four other countries that Gallup has surveyed have scored higher than the U.S.: Lebanon, Peru, Ghana and Nigeria. Go deeper . 

 
 
